1. Generate a separate SSH key per account (if not already done):
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your-email@example.com" -f ~/.ssh/<key-name>
Each account gets its own key file (e.g. id_ed25519 for work, personal for personal).

2. Add each key's public key to the matching GitHub account
pbcopy < ~/.ssh/<key-name>.pub
Paste it under GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG keys for the correct account.

3. Create ~/.ssh/config with a host alias per account
Host github.com
  AddKeysToAgent yes
  UseKeychain yes
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

Host github-personal
  HostName github.com
  User git
  AddKeysToAgent yes
  UseKeychain yes
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/personal
The real host is always github.com; the alias (github-personal) is just a local nickname that tells SSH which key to use.

4. Secure the config file and load the key into the keychain
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config
/usr/bin/ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/personal
Note: use /usr/bin/ssh-add explicitly — if Homebrew's OpenSSH shadows the Apple one, --apple-use-keychain will fail with "unknown option".

5. Test each identity
ssh -T git@github.com          # tests default/work key
ssh -T git@github-personal     # tests the aliased key
A successful response looks like:
Hi <username>! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.
(That message is expected — it means auth worked, not an error.)

6. Use the alias instead of github.com when cloning/setting remotes for that account's repos
git clone git@github-personal:username/repo.git
git remote set-url origin git@github-personal:username/repo.git